WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Spark.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:05.654
When data outgrows one machine, Spark is the standard answer.

3
00:00:05.654 --> 00:00:07.533
Its secret is that it is lazy.

4
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:10.900
Spark is distributed dataframes.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.300
You still filter, group, and aggregate like in pandas, but the data is split across a cluster, and the work is shipped to it.

6
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.467
Here is how a Spark job is built.

7
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:22.933
We read the data.

8
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:24.939
Calling filter does no work.

9
00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:26.767
It just adds a node to a plan.

10
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:28.600
The same goes for groupBy.

11
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.280
And for the aggregation.

12
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.833
Still nothing has computed.

13
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:35.367
Only collect, an action, makes Spark run the whole plan.

14
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:40.533
When the action fires, the driver optimizes the entire plan.

15
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:43.700
Then it fans the work out as tasks to the executors.

16
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.133
Seeing the whole plan first lets Spark push filters down to the file scan and skip columns it never needs.

17
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:53.300
Each executor works on its own slice of the data.

18
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.700
Here it is in code.

19
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.460
These three lines filter, group, and count hot readings.

20
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.067
All lazy, so nothing runs.

21
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.339
The line with collect is the one.

22
00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:06.267
Execution happens there, and not a moment before.

23
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.800
Here is a real use.

24
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:15.733
An exoplanet classifier needs features recomputed over three years of raw files, four hundred gigabytes.

25
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:19.467
A local PySpark session proves the logic on one month.

26
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:26.000
Then the identical script runs on a cluster, because the plan does not care how many executors exist.

27
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.082
Let's recap.

28
00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:30.433
Spark is distributed dataframes.

29
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:33.467
Transformations are lazy and build a plan.

30
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:35.567
An action triggers the whole thing.

31
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:39.192
And laziness lets Spark optimize before it runs.

32
00:01:39.192 --> 00:01:40.300
Next up: Hadoop.
